        "partNumber": 2,
        "title": "Part 2: Memory in Plants",
        "minQ": 14,
        "maxQ": 26,
        "passage": "<h2>Memory in Plants</h2>\n      <p class=\"onmock-passage-subtitle\">How vegetation perceives, learns from, and stores environmental experiences</p>\n    <p><b>A</b> Monica Gagliano of the University of Western Australia tested habituation learning in Mimosa pudica, which closes its leaves on touch. Dropping the plants repeatedly (7 sets of 60 drops), they soon stopped closing their leaves when dropped, recognizing it was harmless, yet still closed tight when given a gentle shake.</p>\n    <p><b>B</b> The mimosa plants retained this habituation three days, six days, and even one month later. In honeybees, a 24-hour memory is considered long-term, showing plants can learn from experience.</p>\n    <p><b>C</b> Vernalization is a well-understood plant memory: plants remember winter cold to time spring flowering. In the 1920s, Soviet scientist Trofim Lysenko chilled seeds to turn winter grains into spring varieties, implanting a false memory of cold.</p>\n    <p><b>D</b> In the 1980s, Mordecai Jaffe found plants remember leaf injury on one side and direct growth to the other side. Plants experiencing drought retain more water when stressed again, broadcasting chemical alerts through air and roots.</p>\n    <p><b>E</b> Richard Amasino at Wisconsin-Madison studied Arabidopsis thaliana. Cold exposure represses the FLC protein that represses flowering genes, allowing flower-promoting proteins to trigger blooming.</p>\n    <p><b>F</b> Peter Crisp argues in Science Advances that forgetting may be a more powerful tool for survival than memory: maintaining epigenetic memories carries metabolic costs that might compromise normal growth.</p>",
        "instruction": "Read the passage carefully and answer Questions 14–26.",
